{"annotations":{"destructiveHint":false,"idempotentHint":true,"openWorldHint":true,"readOnlyHint":true,"title":"Plan a transit journey"},"description":"Plan an origin-to-destination journey. Returns up to three independently validated itineraries (the fastest, then other lines); departure defaults to now. Pass the rider's place names as given: a name for one place (Penn Station, SFO) plans from or to all its stations. A name meaning different places (Broadway) returns the best trip from each, each labelled in reading and explained in notice: use the one the conversation points to, or ask the rider. The search covers every line of the coverage, buses included. When no itinerary is possible (planning unavailable, a search too large, or nothing found) it returns routeHints: lines near each end and direct lines serving both. Call again with routes (the lines you would ride, from routeHints or your own knowledge) to plan along only those lines, which works wherever schedules exist. Present only returned itineraries as confirmed. The whole journey must finish within windowSeconds (default two hours): if none is found for a long trip, retry with a larger window. Each transit leg carries a tripInstanceRef; pass it to get_vehicle_status to see where that train is now. For a wheelchair user or anyone avoiding stairs, set accessibility: \"step_free\": journeys then board, alight and change only at platforms step-free from the street per the agency (partial stations by direction), avoiding stations cut off by a current elevator outage; in-station changes between such platforms are taken as step-free. It refuses with ACCESSIBILITY_UNAVAILABLE when no current elevator report exists and ENDPOINT_NOT_STEP_FREE when an endpoint has no step-free platform now (get_service_status lists the outages). Never present a journey pieced together from get_departures as confirmed; check it here with routes.","inputSchema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","additionalProp